WEBVTT

00:00:00.570 --> 00:00:25.030
<v Ira Glass>Hey there, everybody. Ira Glass here. It's This American Life from WBEZ Chicago. I'm just here for a minute to say we have a special show for you today. One of our producers, Chana Joffe-Walt, has been reporting a lot on education and schools.
<v Ira Glass>大家好，我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。欢迎收听WBEZ芝加哥电台带来的《美国生活》（This American Life）。我占用大家一分钟时间，因为今天我们要播出特别节目。我们的制作人查娜·乔夫-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）一直在深度报道教育和学校相关话题。

00:00:25.030 --> 00:01:00.770
<v Ira Glass>And three years ago, she did this story that's about a part of education that everybody knows about, but I think we do not talk about much at all, not in a way that feels like anything. And this is one of my favorite shows we've ever done and full of surprise turns and twists. And I don't want to waste any more time here setting this up, so I'm just going to hand over the show to Chana so we can get to it. Here she is.
<v Ira Glass>三年前，她做过一个关于教育的专题故事。这是每个人都知道，但我认为我们平时几乎从不谈论的一个方面，至少没有触及到实质。这是我最喜欢的节目之一，充满了意想不到的转折和波澜。我不想再浪费时间做铺垫了，直接把节目交给查娜，让我们进入正题。有请查娜。

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:22.220
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In 2005, Lisa and Angela were part of a program that brought their two schools together, sort of a classroom exchange. Kids from Lisa's school would see Angela's school and vice versa. They thought their students would get a lot out of meeting each other and learning about one another's schools because their students spend their school days in two very different places.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>2005年，丽莎（Lisa）和安吉拉（Angela）参与了一个将她们两所学校联系在一起的项目，算是一种课堂交流活动。丽莎学校的学生会去参观安吉拉的学校，反之亦然。她们认为，学生们通过见面交流和了解对方的学校，会大有裨益，因为学生们在截然不同的两个地方度过学校时光。

00:01:22.220 --> 00:01:35.150
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Lisa Greenbaum's school, University Heights High School, is a public school. It's 97% black and Hispanic. It's located in the poorest congressional district in the country, the South Bronx.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>丽莎·格林鲍姆（Lisa Greenbaum）的学校，大学高地高中（University Heights High School），是一所公立学校。这里97%的学生是黑人和拉丁裔。它位于全国最贫困的国会选区——南布朗克斯区。

00:01:35.150 --> 00:01:54.170
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Angela Vassos' school, Fieldston, is also in the Bronx. But it's one of New York City's elite private schools. It's 70% white. It's known as a progressive school. One in five kids gets financial aid, which is helpful because last year tuition was $43,000.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>安吉拉·瓦索斯（Angela Vassos）的学校，菲尔德斯顿学校（Fieldston），也在布朗克斯，但它是纽约市的精英私立学校之一。那里70%的学生是白人，以进步教育闻名。五分之一的学生获得经济援助，这很有必要，因为去年的学费是43,000美元。

00:01:54.170 --> 00:02:15.560
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>These two schools were three miles from each other, but the students basically needed a foreign exchange program to meet each other. The teachers started by having their students write letters to each other. They were pen pals for a while, which was going well.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这两所学校相距仅三英里，但学生们基本上需要一个交换生项目才能相见。老师们先让他们互通书信。他们做了一段时间笔友，进展还算顺利。

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:30.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And then Lisa and Angela planned a time to get them together. Lisa, the public school teacher, says the moment her kids got off the bus at Fieldston, the private school, they had a dramatic reaction to what they saw.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>后来，丽莎和安吉拉计划了一次面对面的交流。公立学校的老师丽莎说，当她的学生下校车到达菲尔德斯顿私立学校的那一刻，他们对所见之景产生了剧烈的反应。

00:02:30.260 --> 00:02:52.730
<v Lisa Greenbaum>They couldn't believe the campus. They felt like everyone was looking at them. And one of the students started screaming and crying. Like, this is unfair. This is-- I don't want to be here. I'm leaving. I'm leaving right now. I'm going home. We were like, Melanie, it's OK. You should stay with the group. Let's talk, you know?
<v Lisa Greenbaum>他们无法相信那个校园。他们觉得每个人都在盯着他们看。其中一名学生开始尖叫哭泣，说：“这不公平，我不想待在这里，我要走，我现在就要回家。”我们当时就在说：“梅兰妮（Melanie），没事的，你应该和大家待在一起，让我们聊聊，好吗？”

00:02:52.730 --> 00:03:06.370
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Some context, University Heights, the public school these kids were coming from, it's small and friendly. There are no metal detectors. But it's a public school in a poor neighborhood. The kids share a building and a gym with another school.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>补充一点背景：这些孩子所在的大学高地公立学校规模小而友好，没有金属探测器，但这毕竟是一所位于贫困街区的公立学校，学生们还和另一所学校共用教学楼和体育馆。

00:03:06.370 --> 00:03:20.900
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The private school, Fieldston, has an 18-acre campus on a hill. Stone buildings are connected by landscaped paths. Every few windows is the size of a garage door. There's a dance studio, an art gallery, a pool.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>而菲尔德斯顿私立学校拥有一个位于山坡上的18英亩校园，石头建筑由景观小径连接，窗户大得像车库门，还有舞蹈室、艺术画廊和游泳池。

00:03:20.900 --> 00:03:42.530
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So on that first day, the public school kids, almost all of them Puerto Rican and Dominican kids from the South Bronx, walk onto the campus, look around at the quad, the trees, see the school's mission etched into the stone arches. Angela, the private school teacher from Fieldston, came to greet them and immediately noticed that one of the girls seemed upset.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>于是第一天，几乎全部由南布朗克斯区的波多黎各和多米尼加裔孩子组成的公立学校学生，走进校园，看着方院、树木，看着刻在石拱门上的校训。菲尔德斯顿的私立学校老师安吉拉过来迎接他们，立刻注意到其中一个女孩似乎很难过。

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:55.610
<v Angela Vassos>And I went over to talk to her, as did Lisa. And she jumped up and she said, we've got to get out of here. We've got to get out of here. We've got to get out of here. And I thought it was a joke at the beginning. I didn't realize, you know?
<v Angela Vassos>我和丽莎一起走过去跟她说话。她跳起来说：“我们得离开这里，我们必须得离开这儿。”我起初以为她在开玩笑，我没意识到（情况的严重性）。

00:03:55.610 --> 00:04:18.740
<v Angela Vassos>And it really was-- I mean, she went white, like completely white, you know? Blood drained from her face. And her friend was holding her and saying, calm down, Melanie. Calm down, Melanie. So it was just an awful situation. None of us had anticipated such a strong reaction.
<v Angela Vassos>但这确实是……我的意思是，她脸色煞白，完全变得苍白，你知道吗？血色从她脸上褪去。她的朋友抱住她说：“冷静点，梅兰妮，冷静点。”那真是一个可怕的情况。我们谁也没想到会有这么强烈的反应。

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:38.350
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There are hundreds of programs right now that are trying to do some version of this exchange program to help people connect across a growing divide. For a lot of students, American public schools are more segregated than they were a generation ago, not less. The gap between rich and poor is wider than it's been since the Great Depression.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在有成百上千个项目试图进行这种交换项目，帮助人们跨越日益扩大的鸿沟。对许多学生来说，美国的公立学校比一代人之前更加隔离，而不是相反。贫富差距也是自大萧条以来最严重的。

00:04:38.350 --> 00:04:57.730
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Basically, whatever gap you hear about-- income gap, achievement gap, racial divide-- these two groups of kids from University Heights and Fieldston exist on opposite sides. And just seeing across that divide, something so many of us never do, can be incredibly powerful.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>基本上，你听说的任何差距——收入差距、学业成就差距、种族鸿沟——这两组来自大学高地和菲尔德斯顿的孩子正处于鸿沟的两端。仅仅是隔着鸿沟相望，这种我们大多数人从未做过的事情，可能就具有难以置信的冲击力。

00:04:57.730 --> 00:05:19.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>In this case, from the very first few minutes, it was definitely dramatic. This incident when the kids visited Fieldston happened 10 years ago. And I heard this story from so many different people-- the teachers, kids from both schools. Allison Roland was a junior at the private school, Fieldston, at the time. She remembers the girl who freaked out.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在这种情况下，从最初的几分钟开始，过程确实充满戏剧性。孩子们参观菲尔德斯顿的这件事发生在10年前。我从很多人——老师、两所学校的孩子——那里听说了这个故事。艾莉森·罗兰（Allison Roland）当时是菲尔德斯顿私立学校的高二学生。她记得那个崩溃的女孩。

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:24.380
<v Allison Roland>People were like, what happened? That was weird. But nobody really-- it was very behind the scenes.
<v Allison Roland>大家都在问：“发生了什么？太奇怪了。”但没人真的——事情发生在幕后，大家没怎么在意。

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:30.380
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Even more, she remembers the feeling she had seeing the girl freak out, feeling helpless.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>更重要的是，她记得自己看着那个女孩崩溃时感到的那种无助感。

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:51.610
<v Allison Roland>It's uncomfortable when you can't help someone not be uncomfortable. No one wants to feel like they're on the hill school on the top of the hill, and there are people-- it's uncomfortable. But at the same time, I totally understood where she was coming from in terms of it is messed up. It's crazy. Our educations have been so drastically different, and that's completely unfair.
<v Allison Roland>当你无法帮助别人摆脱不适时，这种感觉很糟糕。没人想感觉自己站在山上的学校里，而另一些人……这很令人难受。但同时，我完全理解她为什么会那样，因为那确实很糟糕。这太疯狂了。我们受的教育截然不同，这完全不公平。

00:05:51.610 --> 00:06:04.000
<v Allison Roland>But what do you do in the moment when you're still a high school student, and you don't have the power-- like I don't have a degree. I couldn't become a politician in that moment and change-- I don't know. Making change just takes a long time.
<v Allison Roland>但当你还是个高中生，没有权力的时候，你能做什么？我那时连学位都没有，在那一刻我也无法成为政治家去改变什么……我不知道，做出改变需要很长时间。

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:38.860
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>A lot of Fieldston students do go on to be politicians, and run Walt Disney and the New York Times, and host evening news programs, and design major American cities. And part of the point of programs like these that try to bridge the divide is-- seeing as the private school kids will likely go on to be important, influential people, maybe write education policy or finance new businesses-- it's good for them to know not everybody's life looks like theirs. But of course, then there's the question, what do the public school kids get out of it?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>很多菲尔德斯顿的学生后来确实成为了政治家，经营华特迪士尼或《纽约时报》，主持晚间新闻节目，设计美国主要城市。而这类试图弥合鸿沟的项目目的之一是——鉴于私立学校的孩子未来很可能成为重要且有影响力的人，或许会制定教育政策或资助新商业——让他们知道并非每个人的生活都和他们一样，这很有益。但当然，随后就产生了一个问题：公立学校的孩子能从中得到什么？

00:06:38.860 --> 00:07:01.690
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Right now, there's a popular idea in education-- it pops up all over the place-- about exposure, that exposure is particularly important for poor kids-- not just important, that it can change destinies. You know, you take a group of kids to tour a college campus, they'll be more likely to go to college. Or if you just know someone who went to college, that'll help.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>现在，教育界有一个流行的想法——到处都在出现——关于“接触”，认为接触对贫困孩子尤为重要，不仅是重要，甚至能改变命运。你知道，带一群孩子参观大学校园，他们上大学的可能性就会增加；或者如果你认识一个上过大学的人，那也会有帮助。

00:07:01.690 --> 00:07:18.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The idea is if you want a kid to move from one social class to another, that kid has to see what it looks like over there on the other side. Exposure is a tool for social change and economic mobility. Or it just sucks.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>其逻辑是，如果你想让一个孩子从一个社会阶层跨越到另一个阶层，这个孩子必须亲眼看到另一边是什么样。接触是社会变革和经济流动的工具。或者，那也可能很糟。

00:07:18.330 --> 00:07:35.670
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You see how much you did not get, and it's shocking or painful. And you freak out, like that one girl, Melanie. The people who run programs like these hope that even if it's upsetting in the moment, ultimately, this kind of experience helps more than it hurts.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你看到了你缺少了多少，这很令人震惊或痛苦。然后你就会崩溃，像那个女孩梅兰妮一样。那些负责这些项目的人希望，即使当时让人难受，但最终这种经历的帮助大于伤害。

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:52.620
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But nobody really knows because this is the kind of thing that plays out over time. It occurred to me that the group of kids who were part of that first year exchange between Fieldston and University Heights could answer this question. Does it hurt or help the public school kids?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但没人知道，因为这类事情是随着时间推移才显现的。我想到，那第一年参加菲尔德斯顿和大学高地交换项目的孩子们或许能回答这个问题：这到底是伤害了还是帮助了公立学校的孩子？

00:07:52.620 --> 00:08:20.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>It's been 10 years. They're in their mid-20s now, so they've gone to college, gotten apartments and jobs. And it turns out this one experience really has shaped some of the public school kids in profound ways, ways I did not see coming. Today's show, what happens when you see the other side, and it looks a lot better?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>十年过去了。他们现在二十多岁了，上了大学，有了公寓和工作。事实证明，这次经历确实以深刻的方式影响了一些公立学校的孩子，这种方式是我没预料到的。今天的节目将探讨：当你看到另一边的世界，而且发现它看起来好得多时，会发生什么？

00:08:20.260 --> 00:08:44.410
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Let's begin with Melanie, the girl who freaked out. I wondered about Melanie because she had such a strong, immediate reaction that everyone remembered, but also because listen to what happened after that. So apparently, Melanie started crying the moment she got to Fieldston, wanted to leave. But the teachers managed to calm her down. And for a while, it seemed like the moment had passed, and they were fine.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>先从那个崩溃的女孩梅兰妮说起。我对梅兰妮感到好奇，因为她有着每个人都记忆犹新的强烈即时反应，而且听听之后发生了什么。显然，梅兰妮在到达菲尔德斯顿的那一刻就开始哭泣，想离开。但老师们设法让她冷静了下来。有那么一会儿，事情似乎过去了，他们表现得还行。

00:08:44.410 --> 00:09:00.960
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>They partnered all the students up, so the University Heights kids could go with the Fieldston kids to some of their classes. Marlena Edelstein, a Fieldston student, told me the girl who shadowed her, she doesn't remember her name. But other people told me it was Melanie. Marlena has never forgotten her.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>他们让所有学生两两组队，大学高地的孩子可以跟着菲尔德斯顿的孩子去上课。玛琳娜·埃德尔斯坦（Marlena Edelstein）是菲尔德斯顿的学生，她告诉我那个跟她一起上课的女孩，她不记得名字了，但其他人告诉我那是梅兰妮。玛琳娜从未忘记她。

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:28.770
<v Marlena Edelstein>I remember we were studying the Enlightenment. And we were looking at it through paintings. And I remember her making connections between the painting and Enlightenment era philosophers, and quoting John Locke, and just schooling all of us-- you know, making connections that I would have been completely incapable of making, and just clearly the smartest person in the room.
<v Marlena Edelstein>我记得我们当时在学启蒙运动，通过绘画来观察它。我记得她把这幅画和启蒙时代的哲学家联系起来，引用约翰·洛克的话，简直是在给我们上课……那种联系能力是我完全无法做到的，她显然是房间里最聪明的人。

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:52.890
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Then, after wowing everyone in this class with her intellect, Melanie left, ran away. She found the experience of being at Fieldston so upsetting she ultimately could not stand to be there. And Lisa Greenbaum, her teacher, told me Melanie did not just run away from Fieldston that day. She also seemed to run away from high school. She doesn't remember seeing her much after that.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>然后，在以智慧惊艳全班后，梅兰妮离开了，跑掉了。她发现待在菲尔德斯顿的经历令她极其痛苦，以至于最终无法忍受待在那里。她的老师丽莎·格林鲍姆告诉我，梅兰妮那天不只是从菲尔德斯顿跑掉了，她似乎还从高中生活里“跑掉”了。她不记得在那之后见过她多少次。

00:09:52.890 --> 00:10:03.900
<v Lisa Greenbaum>I would love to know-- I would love to know how she feels about who she is and if that moment in her life in any way shaped the way she thinks today.
<v Lisa Greenbaum>我很想知道，我很想知道她如何看待自己，以及生命中的那一刻是否以某种方式塑造了她今天的思考方式。

00:10:03.900 --> 00:10:12.450
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Lisa told me she had no idea where Melanie was. But she said, you know who you should call? Ashleigh Wallace. That was Melanie's best friend. She'll know what happened.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>丽莎告诉我她根本不知道梅兰妮在哪里。但她说：“你知道你应该给谁打电话吗？阿什莉·华莱士（Ashleigh Wallace）。那是梅兰妮最好的朋友。她会知道发生了什么。”

00:10:12.450 --> 00:10:15.670
<v Ashleigh Wallace>So me and Melanie were pissed off.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>我和梅兰妮当时气坏了。

00:10:15.670 --> 00:10:16.550
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>What happened?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>发生了什么？

00:10:16.550 --> 00:10:21.900
<v Ashleigh Wallace>We were definitely pissed off. I mean have you ever seen Edward Scissorhands?
<v Ashleigh Wallace>我们绝对气坏了。你看过《剪刀手爱德华》吗？

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:22.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Seen what?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>看过什么？

00:10:22.850 --> 00:10:24.180
<v Ashleigh Wallace>Edward Scissorhands.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>《剪刀手爱德华》。

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:25.250
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Yeah, yeah.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>哦，是的，看过。

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:46.440
<v Ashleigh Wallace>And you know how his house is at the top of the hill, and it's all dark and gloomy? And then you have the rest of the town where all the houses are colorful, and it's sunny all day and bright, or whatever the case is? It was just like that. Like we were leaving the Bronx and going into some complete, totally different, Utopian existence.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>你知道他的房子在山顶上，又暗又阴沉吗？然后镇上的其他地方，房子都很鲜艳，阳光明媚，很亮，不管情况如何？当时就是那样。就像我们离开了布朗克斯，进入了一个完全不同的乌托邦世界。

00:10:46.440 --> 00:11:05.170
<v Ashleigh Wallace>You know, Lisa kept telling us while we were pen palling these kids that, oh, they go to a private school, but they're just like us. And just like us is you live in a bad neighborhood like we do. You go to a bullshit, shitty ass school like we do where we have no cafeteria because it's been converted into a classroom.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>你知道，丽莎在我们通信时一直对我们说：“噢，他们去的是私立学校，但他们和我们一样。”而“和我们一样”的意思是：你住在这个像我们一样糟糕的社区；你上着像我们一样烂透了的学校，甚至连食堂都没有，因为被改成了教室。

00:11:05.170 --> 00:11:19.260
<v Ashleigh Wallace>We have a daycare for the mothers who have children. There's-- what? One, two, three floors in our school? She's like, oh, they're just like us. They're nothing like us, nothing at all.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>我们学校还有给有孩子的妈妈们准备的托儿所。学校……有什么？三层楼？她竟然说“他们和我们一样”。他们和我们一点都不像，完全不像。

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:22.570
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I asked Ashleigh, so where is Melanie now?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我问阿什莉：“那么梅兰妮现在在哪里？”

00:11:22.570 --> 00:11:32.050
<v Ashleigh Wallace>She actually graduated a little earlier. And I've been looking for her since then because, you know, me and her used to be very, very close.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>她其实早一点就毕业了。从那以后我一直在找她，因为你知道，我和她曾经非常非常亲近。

00:11:32.050 --> 00:11:41.970
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>For a while, it seemed like maybe they just lost touch. That happens after high school. Ashleigh's mom moved to Atlanta. Eventually, Ashleigh did too and had a baby.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有段时间，似乎他们只是失去了联系，高中毕业后常发生这种事。阿什莉的妈妈搬到了亚特兰大，后来阿什莉也搬去了，还生了个孩子。

00:11:41.970 --> 00:11:56.420
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But after a while, it stopped feeling to Ashleigh like the two of them had just drifted apart and started to feel more like Melanie did not want to be found. Her phone number changed. She's nowhere online. Ashleigh has checked Facebook so many times.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但过了一阵子，在阿什莉看来，这感觉不再只是两个人渐行渐远，而更像是梅兰妮不想被找到。她的电话号码换了，网上也找不到人。阿什莉查过很多次Facebook。

00:11:56.420 --> 00:12:00.940
<v Ashleigh Wallace>My mom always asks about her and stuff, but I could not find her.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>我妈妈总是问起她之类的事，但我找不到她。

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:02.790
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Do you know if she went to college?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你知道她上大学了吗？

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:05.220
<v Ashleigh Wallace>No, I really don't.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>不，我真的不知道。

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:23.940
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>But Ashleigh says she must have. How else does someone disappear like that unless they make it out of the Bronx and never look back? There is a New York City number listed under Melanie's name, but of course--
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>但阿什莉说她肯定上了。除了逃离布朗克斯且永远不再回头，一个人怎么可能消失成那样呢？在梅兰妮名下有一个纽约市的电话号码，但当然……

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:29.520
<v Operator>--does not accept calls at this time. We're sorry for any inconvenience this may cause.
<v Operator>“……该号码目前不接受来电。由此带来的不便我们深表歉意。”

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:53.300
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>There's a woman with the same name who manages a real estate firm in Corpus Christi. There's a dental lab technician in Harrisburg, an executive office professional in Las Vegas, a woman on Twitter, who I think lives in Mexico and has devoted her account to retweeting Justin Bieber's Twitter account. They all seem to be about the right age, all not her.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>有个同名女子在科珀斯克里斯蒂市经营一家房地产公司；还有个在哈里斯堡工作的牙科实验室技师；拉斯维加斯的一名行政人员；推特上还有一个我想是住在墨西哥的女性，她的账户专门转发贾斯汀·比伯的推文。他们看起来年纪都差不多，但都不是她。

00:12:53.300 --> 00:13:09.030
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>A background check on Melanie's name lists a current and former address. I went to the current one. She does not live there. Sent letters to both-- no luck. Ashleigh, her friend from high school, gave me a couple of names of people she thought might have kept up with Melanie.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>对梅兰妮名字的背景调查列出了当前和以前的地址。我去过现在的地址，她不在那儿。给两个地址都寄了信，都没有回音。她在高中的朋友阿什莉给了我几个可能还在和梅兰妮联系的人的名字。

00:13:09.030 --> 00:13:20.700
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And top of the list was Pablo Muriel, their high school history teacher. He's Puerto Rican, like Melanie, from the neighborhood. Ashleigh says, we all loved Pablo, but especially Melanie.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>名单上排第一的是巴勃罗·穆里尔（Pablo Muriel），他们高中的历史老师。他是波多黎各人，和梅兰妮来自同一个街区。阿什莉说，我们都爱巴勃罗，但梅兰妮最爱。

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:21.690
<v Pablo Muriel>Yes, hello?
<v Pablo Muriel>喂，你好？

00:13:21.690 --> 00:13:24.060
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Hi. It's Chana Joffe-Walt, the reporter.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你好，我是记者查娜·乔夫-沃尔特。

00:13:24.060 --> 00:13:31.600
<v Pablo Muriel>Oh, yes, hi. How are you? You caught me at the perfect time. These people calling me into meetings. I was like, oh, thank god. How are you?
<v Pablo Muriel>噢，你好。你好吗？你打来的时间太巧了。那些人正拉着我去开会，我都快烦死了，感谢老天。你好吗？

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:42.510
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Pablo said Melanie is one of those kids you remember because she was very, very, very smart-- three verys-- and full of potential. She stood out to him for other reasons too.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>巴勃罗说梅兰妮是那种你会记住的孩子，因为她非常非常非常聪明——用了三个“非常”——而且充满潜力。她因为其他原因也让他印象深刻。

00:13:42.510 --> 00:13:57.820
<v Pablo Muriel>She was a bit out there, like her personality was out there. However, her intellect surpassed most of her peers at the time. Interesting enough, I think she passed by the school to visit maybe four years ago.
<v Pablo Muriel>她有点另类，性格很外向。然而，她的智力在当时超过了大多数同龄人。有趣的是，我想她大约四年前曾路过学校，回来参观过。

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:14.550
<v Pablo Muriel>And I remember her coming back with me to say hello to me. And I was with another teacher, sitting down-- I won't forget this day because it was just so odd. She came and spoke to me. And I told her, hold on. I'll talk to you in a minute. I think she took it as me brushing her off, and she just kind of stormed out. And I never saw her again.
<v Pablo Muriel>我记得她回来跟我打招呼，我当时正和另一位老师坐着——那天我永远不会忘，因为太奇怪了。她过来跟我说话，我告诉她：“等等，我一会儿跟你说。”我想她以为我在敷衍她，她就那样气呼呼地走了。我再也没见过她。

00:14:14.550 --> 00:14:18.660
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>You were just busy and like, happy to see you, just hang on a second?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>你当时只是太忙，而且很高兴见到她，让她等一会儿？

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:31.260
<v Pablo Muriel>Yeah. Yeah, because it was she caught me between classes. Hi, Pablo. I'm like, oh, hey, Melanie. Hold on. I'll talk to you in a minute. And then she just said, OK, whatever. And she just left. And I never saw her again.
<v Pablo Muriel>是的，是的，因为她是课间把我截住了。她说“嗨，巴勃罗”，我当时说“噢，嘿，梅兰妮，等等，我一会儿跟你说”。然后她就说“好吧，随便”，然后就走了。我再也没见过她。

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:44.910
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I began contacting former classmates. And I became sort of obsessed because it was weird how invisible she was. Everyone else was super easy to find. These are 25-year-olds, and we have the internet.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我开始联系以前的同学。我变得有些着迷，因为她消失得如此彻底，这很奇怪。其他人都很容易找到。他们都是25岁的人，而且我们有互联网。

00:14:44.910 --> 00:15:04.260
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And the classmates' memories of Melanie were all similarly mythic. She spoke her mind. She was brilliant. She was fierce. She was going to make it. And no one had heard from her.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>同学对梅兰妮的记忆都带有某种神话色彩。她直言不讳，非常聪明，极其犀利。她本来会成功的。但没一个人有她的消息。

00:15:04.260 --> 00:15:26.330
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Weirdly, the one person I contacted who had a real idea of what happened to Melanie was the teacher from Fieldston, the private school, Angela Vassos. Someone told me Angela had taken a special interest in Melanie after she got so upset at Fieldston. And a decade later, when I met Angela and first asked about Melanie, she started crying.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>奇怪的是，我联系到的唯一一个真正知道梅兰妮发生了什么的人，是菲尔德斯顿私立学校的老师安吉拉·瓦索斯。有人告诉我，在梅兰妮从菲尔德斯顿回来后变得那么沮丧，安吉拉对她产生了特殊的兴趣。十年后，当我见到安吉拉第一次问起梅兰妮时，她哭了。

00:15:26.330 --> 00:15:28.300
<v Angela Vassos>I'm sorry. It's just Melanie's--
<v Angela Vassos>对不起，只是梅兰妮她……

00:15:28.300 --> 00:15:28.850
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>No problem.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>没关系。

00:15:28.850 --> 00:15:32.100
<v Angela Vassos>[INAUDIBLE], you know? There's a part of me I guess--
<v Angela Vassos>（听不清），你知道吗？我想我内心有一部分……

00:15:32.100 --> 00:15:37.380
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Angela got a box of tissues, then sat down.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>安吉拉拿了一盒纸巾，然后坐下。

00:15:37.380 --> 00:15:51.690
<v Angela Vassos>I had a real soft spot for that kid. I still have a soft spot for that kid. She was smart, really smart. I don't know if she realized. She knew she was powerful, but I don't know if she knew she was smart.
<v Angela Vassos>我对那孩子有一种特别的偏爱。我现在依然如此。她很聪明，真的非常聪明。我不知道她自己有没有意识到。她知道自己有影响力，但我不知道她是否知道自己很聪明。

00:15:51.690 --> 00:16:03.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>After that first encounter at Fieldston, Angela tried hard to gain Melanie's trust. I think she felt partially responsible. And she really wanted to help Melanie, even though she wasn't Melanie's teacher.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>在那次菲尔德斯顿的初次相遇后，安吉拉极力想赢得梅兰妮的信任。我想她觉得有部分责任。虽然她不是梅兰妮的老师，但她真心想帮助梅兰妮。

00:16:03.990 --> 00:16:26.380
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The program kept going throughout the year. Angela would bring her private school students over to the public school. And sometimes she'd visit on her own. And every time, she'd make a special point to check in on Melanie. The last time Angela ever saw Melanie was in the public school. Angela was over there visiting, and Lillian, the college advisor, comes in.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>那个项目全年都在进行。安吉拉会把她的私立学校学生带到公立学校。有时她会独自去参观。每一次，她都会特意去看看梅兰妮。安吉拉最后一次见到梅兰妮是在那所公立学校里。当时安吉拉在那里访问，负责大学咨询的莉莲（Lillian）走了进来。

00:16:26.380 --> 00:16:46.990
<v Angela Vassos>She walked in. And Melanie was really on a tear about white bitches and they don't know anything about the world. And Lillian walks in, and she waves a piece of paper. And she says, Melanie, come here. Melanie goes up. There are like 50 people in the room.
<v Angela Vassos>她走了进来。梅兰妮当时正因为“那些白人贱人一无所知”而大发雷霆。莉莲走了进来，挥着一张纸说：“梅兰妮，过来。”梅兰妮走过去。房间里有50人。

00:16:46.990 --> 00:17:09.560
<v Angela Vassos>She says, Middlebury accepted you, full scholarship. And Melanie started crying. I started crying. We all did. We were so excited. And Lillian, she says, we made it. We made it. We made it. You know? Incredible news. And Middlebury, we're talking about a serious, serious school-- full scholarship.
<v Angela Vassos>她说：“明德学院录取你了，全额奖学金。”梅兰妮开始哭，我也开始哭，大家都哭了。我们太激动了。莉莲说：“我们做到了，我们做到了！”你知道吗？难以置信的消息。而且明德学院（Middlebury），那可是所非常非常厉害的学校，全额奖学金。

00:17:09.560 --> 00:17:35.860
<v Angela Vassos>And Melanie buried her face in Lillian's shoulder here. And they hugged for such a long time, not one of these hugs. It must have been a two-minute hug. And Lillian was swinging her around. And Melanie had her arms around her. And I thought, that's so great. And you knew at that moment-- it was moving because you knew at that moment Melanie felt safe.
<v Angela Vassos>梅兰妮把脸埋在莉莲肩膀里。她们拥抱了很久，不是那种敷衍的拥抱。一定持续了两分钟。莉莲转着圈抱着她，梅兰妮也环抱着她。我想，这太棒了。你在那一刻就知道——这一幕很感人，因为你知道在那一刻梅兰妮感到了安全。

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:49.540
<v Angela Vassos>And that's when Melanie disappeared. Because she did not finish the last month of the year. She disappeared. To this day, they don't know where she is. She probably just got scared. You know?
<v Angela Vassos>然后梅兰妮就消失了。因为她没有完成那一年的最后一个月。她消失了。直到今天，他们都不知道她在哪里。她可能只是被吓坏了，你知道吗？

00:17:49.540 --> 00:17:53.050
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Wait. She got into Middlebury, but she didn't go?
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>等等。她被明德学院录取了，但没去？

00:17:53.050 --> 00:17:57.930
<v Angela Vassos>She didn't finish the last month of school either. She left.
<v Angela Vassos>她连高中的最后一个月都没念完。她离开了。

00:17:57.930 --> 00:17:59.280
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Oh, wow.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>噢，天哪。

00:17:59.280 --> 00:18:10.090
<v Angela Vassos>Yeah. Literally disappeared. No one could find her.
<v Angela Vassos>是的。真的消失了。没人找得到她。

00:18:10.090 --> 00:18:27.340
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I called Middlebury to see if maybe Melanie did attend. They said, no. Did she get in? They said, we have no record showing that she received an offer of admission. I didn't know if that meant she wasn't admitted, or they just had no record either way, but that's all they would say.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我打电话给明德学院，想查查梅兰妮是否真的去读过。他们说：“没有。”“那她被录取了吗？”他们说：“我们没有任何关于她收到录取通知的记录。”我不知道这是否意味着她没有被录取，或者是他们确实没有任何记录，但他们只肯说这么多。

00:18:27.340 --> 00:18:41.990
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>And this was strange because Angela had such a vivid memory of Melanie getting in. And Angela herself went to Middlebury, so she probably didn't mix up the name of the college. Plus, the college advisor at Melanie's school seemed to remember it.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>这很奇怪，因为安吉拉对梅兰妮被录取这件事记忆如此清晰，而且安吉拉本人就是明德学院毕业的，所以她不太可能搞错大学的名字。此外，梅兰妮学校的大学顾问似乎也记得这件事。

00:18:41.990 --> 00:18:59.740
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>So at this point, I went back to Melanie's high school friend, Ashleigh, and just asked if it sounded like Melanie to psych herself out like that. And she said, no way. I only made it to my GED, but Melanie was singularly focused on college. She'd curse me out when I cut class.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>所以这时候，我回去找了梅兰妮的高中好友阿什莉，问她梅兰妮是否会像那样自乱阵脚。她说：“绝对不会。我连高中毕业证都没拿到，但梅兰妮是铁了心要上大学的。当我逃课时，她会骂我。”

00:18:59.740 --> 00:19:05.890
<v Ashleigh Wallace>When it came down to me and her, I was what she was trying to avoid.
<v Ashleigh Wallace>当我和她在一起时，我是她试图避免变成的样子。

00:19:05.890 --> 00:19:15.010
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Melanie was not going to fail at high school. She would go to college with no babies and live far, far from New York City, period.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>梅兰妮绝不会在高中失败。她要去读大学，不生孩子，住在离纽约市很远很远的地方，句号。

00:19:15.010 --> 00:19:22.550
<v Siri>You can say things like, when is my recycling picked up? Or report a noisy neighbor. Go ahead.
<v Siri>你可以说些话，比如：垃圾什么时候回收？或者举报吵闹的邻居。请讲。

00:19:22.550 --> 00:19:24.440
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>Death record.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>死亡记录。

00:19:24.440 --> 00:19:28.610
<v Siri>All right. I can help you learn how to request a certificate for a death reported in the city.
<v Siri>好的，我可以教你如何申请报告在该市发生的死亡证明。

00:19:28.610 --> 00:19:47.190
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I didn't think Melanie was dead, but a friend asked. And once that seed was planted, I could not get the idea out of my head. The same day I went in person to the city's office for vital records, I also went to an old address listed on a background check, one I'd sent letters to.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我不认为梅兰妮死了，但有个朋友问过。一旦那个念头种下，我就挥之不去。去市政府获取人口记录的那天，我也去了背景调查里列出的一个旧地址，就是我寄过信的那个。

00:19:47.190 --> 00:19:49.160
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>I don't think she lives here anymore.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>我想她不在这里住了。

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:58.040
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>The buzzer didn't have Melanie's name on it, and I pressed it anyway. No answer. Stood by the door to the building.
<v Chana Joffe-Walt>门铃上没有梅兰妮的名字，但我还是按了。没人回答。我站在楼门口。

